{% extends 'base.html.twig' %}
{% block title %}User Guide – DHCP Management{% endblock %}

{% block content %}
{% set section_title = 'DHCP Management' %}
{{ include('user_guide/_breadcrumb.html.twig') }}

<div class="d-flex justify-content-between align-items-center mb-4">
    <h1 class="h3 mb-0"><i class="bi bi-hdd-network me-2"></i>DHCP Management</h1>
    <a href="{{ path('dhcp_server_index') }}" class="btn btn-sm btn-outline-primary">
        <i class="bi bi-arrow-right-circle me-1"></i>Go to DHCP Servers
    </a>
</div>

<div class="row g-4">
<div class="col-lg-9">

<div class="card mb-4" id="overview">
    <div class="card-header fw-semibold"><i class="bi bi-info-circle me-1"></i>Overview</div>
    <div class="card-body">
        <p>DashDDI generates <a href="https://kea.isc.org" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Kea DHCP</a> configuration from your subnet and host data, then deploys it to your DHCP servers via SSH. It also receives lease notifications from Kea via a webhook so you can track current and historical lease activity.</p>
        <p class="mb-0">The flow is: <strong>Subnets &amp; hosts</strong> → <strong>Config generation</strong> → <strong>SSH deploy</strong> → <strong>Kea reload</strong> → <strong>Lease notifications back to DashDDI</strong>.</p>
    </div>
</div>

<div class="card mb-4" id="dhcp-servers">
    <div class="card-header fw-semibold"><i class="bi bi-server me-1"></i>DHCP Servers</div>
    <div class="card-body">
        <p>Register your Kea DHCP servers at <strong>Servers → Servers</strong> (DHCP section) or via the <strong>Servers</strong> overview. Each server needs:</p>
        <dl class="row mb-0">
            <dt class="col-sm-3">Name <span class="text-danger">*</span></dt>
            <dd class="col-sm-9">A label for this server, e.g. <code>kea-primary</code>.</dd>
            <dt class="col-sm-3">Hostname</dt>
            <dd class="col-sm-9">The IP or DNS name DashDDI uses to SSH into this server.</dd>
            <dt class="col-sm-3">SSH Username</dt>
            <dd class="col-sm-9">The Linux user account used for the SSH connection (must have permission to write Kea config and reload the service).</dd>
            <dt class="col-sm-3">SSH Public Key</dt>
            <dd class="col-sm-9">Generated automatically by DashDDI. Copy the displayed key to <code>~/.ssh/authorized_keys</code> on the DHCP server.</dd>
            <dt class="col-sm-3">Config Path</dt>
            <dd class="col-sm-9">Path on the remote server where DashDDI should write the generated Kea config file.</dd>
            <dt class="col-sm-3">Reload Command</dt>
            <dd class="col-sm-9">Shell command to reload Kea after the config is uploaded, e.g. <code>sudo systemctl reload kea-dhcp4</code>.</dd>
        </dl>
        <p class="mt-3 mb-0 text-muted small">See the <a href="{{ path('user_guide_integrations') }}#dhcp-integration">DHCP integration setup guide</a> for the full server-side configuration steps.</p>
    </div>
</div>

<div class="card mb-4" id="config-generation">
    <div class="card-header fw-semibold"><i class="bi bi-file-earmark-code me-1"></i>Configuration Generation</div>
    <div class="card-body">
        <p>DashDDI generates Kea DHCP4 configuration by combining data from:</p>
        <ul>
            <li><strong>Subnets</strong> assigned to the DHCP server (via the subnet's DHCP server field), including their gateway, DNS views, and address blocks</li>
            <li><strong>Host interfaces</strong> — any interface with a MAC address and a static IP gets a <code>host reservation</code> entry</li>
        </ul>
        <p>The generated config includes:</p>
        <ul class="mb-0">
            <li>A <code>subnet4</code> block for each subnet (CIDR, gateway, pools derived from address blocks)</li>
            <li>A <code>reservations</code> section with one entry per host interface that has a static IP</li>
            <li>Global options for lease time, DNS servers, and domain name</li>
        </ul>
    </div>
</div>

<div class="card mb-4" id="push-dhcp">
    <div class="card-header fw-semibold"><i class="bi bi-cloud-upload me-1"></i>Pushing DHCP Configuration</div>
    <div class="card-body">
        <p>Push the generated config to your Kea server in two ways:</p>
        <ol class="mb-0">
            <li><strong>Manually</strong> — navigate to the DHCP server in <strong>Servers → Servers</strong> and click <strong>Push Config</strong></li>
            <li><strong>Scheduled</strong> — set up a cron-based push in <strong>Other Settings → Scheduler</strong></li>
        </ol>
        <p class="mt-2 mb-0">The push uploads the config file to the configured path on the DHCP server, then runs the reload command. Execution output is visible in <strong>Servers → Push Logs</strong>.</p>
    </div>
</div>

<div class="card mb-4" id="leases">
    <div class="card-header fw-semibold"><i class="bi bi-journal-text me-1"></i>DHCP Leases</div>
    <div class="card-body">
        <p>Browse active and historical DHCP leases at <strong>DHCP → DHCP Leases</strong>. When Kea is configured to notify DashDDI (see below), lease records are stored automatically whenever a lease is granted or renewed.</p>
        <p>The lease list shows:</p>
        <ul class="mb-2">
            <li>IP address, MAC address, hostname reported by the client</li>
            <li>Subnet the lease belongs to</li>
            <li>Lease start and expiry times</li>
            <li>Whether the lease is currently active</li>
        </ul>
        <p class="mb-0">The host list and host detail pages show the <strong>current active lease</strong> for each interface's MAC address alongside any static IP — making it easy to see if a device is getting the expected address.</p>
    </div>
</div>

<div class="card mb-4" id="kea-lease-notifications">
    <div class="card-header fw-semibold"><i class="bi bi-arrow-left-right me-1"></i>Configuring Kea to Send Lease Notifications</div>
    <div class="card-body">
        <p>Kea can be configured to POST lease events to DashDDI whenever an address is granted or renewed. This requires <strong>Kea 2.2 or later</strong> and uses the <code>run_script</code> hook library.</p>

        <h6>Step 1 — Create an API token</h6>
        <p>Go to <strong>Other Settings → API Tokens</strong>, create a new token, and grant it permission for the <code>POST /api/dhcp/lease</code> route. Copy the token value — it is shown only once.</p>

        <h6>Step 2 — Create the notify script on the Kea server</h6>
        <p>Create <code>/etc/kea/notify-dashddi.sh</code> on the Kea server and make it executable (<code>chmod +x</code>):</p>
        <pre class="bg-body-secondary rounded p-3 small mb-3"><code>#!/bin/bash
# Called by Kea run_script hook on lease4_select and lease4_renew events
if [[ "$LEASES4_AT0_ADDRESS" == "" ]]; then exit 0; fi

curl -s -X POST https://dashddi.example.com/api/dhcp/lease \
  -H "Authorization: Bearer &lt;YOUR_API_TOKEN&gt;" \
  -H "Content-Type: application/json" \
  -d "{
    \"ip-address\": \"$LEASES4_AT0_ADDRESS\",
    \"hw-address\": \"$LEASES4_AT0_HWADDR\",
    \"hostname\": \"$LEASES4_AT0_HOSTNAME\",
    \"expire\": $LEASES4_AT0_CLTT
  }"</code></pre>
        <p>Replace <code>dashddi.example.com</code> with your DashDDI hostname and <code>&lt;YOUR_API_TOKEN&gt;</code> with the token created above.</p>

        <h6>Step 3 — Add the hook to <code>kea-dhcp4.conf</code></h6>
        <p>Add the <code>run_script</code> hook library to the <code>hooks-libraries</code> array in your Kea DHCPv4 configuration:</p>
        <pre class="bg-body-secondary rounded p-3 small mb-3"><code>"hooks-libraries": [
    {
        "library": "/usr/lib/kea/hooks/libdhcp_run_script.so",
        "parameters": {
            "name": "/etc/kea/notify-dashddi.sh",
            "sync": false
        }
    }
]</code></pre>
        <p>The <code>sync: false</code> setting lets Kea process leases without waiting for the script to complete, so a slow or unavailable DashDDI instance does not delay DHCP responses.</p>

        <h6>Step 4 — Reload Kea</h6>
        <p class="mb-3">Reload or restart the Kea DHCPv4 service to apply the hook configuration:</p>
        <pre class="bg-body-secondary rounded p-3 small mb-3"><code>sudo systemctl reload kea-dhcp4</code></pre>

        <h6>DHCPv6</h6>
        <p class="mb-0">The same pattern applies for DHCPv6. Use the <code>LEASES6_AT0_*</code> environment variables in the script (<code>LEASES6_AT0_ADDRESS</code>, <code>LEASES6_AT0_HWADDR</code>, etc.) and add the hook to <code>kea-dhcp6.conf</code> instead.</p>
    </div>
</div>

<div class="card mb-4" id="clearpass-auth-logs">
    <div class="card-header fw-semibold"><i class="bi bi-shield-lock me-1"></i>ClearPass Authentication Logs</div>
    <div class="card-body">
        <p>If ClearPass is configured, authentication events are available at <strong>DHCP → ClearPass Auth Logs</strong>. Each entry shows the MAC address, username, IP address, and authentication outcome for network access requests.</p>
        <p class="mb-0 text-muted small">See the <a href="{{ path('user_guide_integrations') }}#clearpass">ClearPass integration guide</a> for setup details.</p>
    </div>
</div>

</div>{# /col-lg-9 #}

<div class="col-lg-3 d-none d-lg-block">
    <div class="card sticky-top" style="top:80px">
        <div class="card-header small fw-semibold">On this page</div>
        <div class="list-group list-group-flush small">
            <a href="#overview" class="list-group-item list-group-item-action py-1">Overview</a>
